  "Question": "Tom’s age is \\(T\\) years, which is also the sum of the ages of his three children. His age \\(N\\) years ago was twice the sum of their ages then. What is \\(\\frac{T}{N}?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(3\\)",
    "C": "\\(4\\)",
    "D": "\\(5\\)",
    "E": "\\(6\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Going back \\(N\\) years subtracts \\(N\\) from each child's age, so it subtracts \\(3N\\) from their combined age. Their current combined age is \\(T\\), making their earlier combined age \\(T-3N\\). Tom's earlier age was \\(T-N\\).\n\nThe statement that his age then was twice their combined age gives\n\\[\nT-N=2(T-3N)=2T-6N.\n\\]\nRearranging yields \\(T=5N\\). Tom's age is positive, so this also gives \\(N=T/5>0\\), allowing division by \\(N\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{T}{N}=5},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
